[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Meta-programming in Angular"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Meta-programming is a programming technique where a program can manipulate other programs (or itself) as data. In Angular, meta-programming is primarily achieved through decorators, which are a form of syntactic meta-programming."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Decorators"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Decorators are a TypeScript feature (also available in JavaScript with experimental support) that provide a way to add both annotations and a meta-programming syntax for class declarations and members. They use the @expression form, where 'expression' must evaluate to a function that will be called at runtime with information about the decorated declaration."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Types of Decorators in Angular"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Class Decorators: Applied to classes (e.g., @Component, @Directive, @Injectable, @NgModule)."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Property Decorators: Applied to properties of a class (e.g., @Input, @Output)."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Method Decorators: Applied to methods of a class (e.g., @HostListener)."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Parameter Decorators: Applied to parameters of a class constructor (e.g., @Inject)."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Using @Component Decorator"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"The @Component decorator is a class decorator that marks a class as an Angular component and provides metadata that determines how the component should be processed, instantiated, and used at runtime."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"import { Component } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-my-component',
  template: '<div>My Component</div>',
  styleUrls: ['./my-component.component.css']
})
export class MyComponent {
  // Component logic here
}"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, @Component is a decorator function. When the Angular compiler processes this code, it uses the metadata provided in the decorator (selector, template, styleUrls) to generate the necessary code for creating and managing the component."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Using @Input Decorator"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"The @Input decorator is a property decorator that marks a class property as an input property, allowing data to be passed from a parent component to a child component."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"import { Component, Input } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-child',
  template: '<p>Value from parent: {{ inputValue }}</p>'
})
export class ChildComponent {
  @Input() inputValue: string;
}"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, @Input() tells Angular that the 'inputValue' property can receive data from the parent component. In the parent component's template, you would bind a value to this input property using property binding: '<app-child [inputValue]="parentValue"></app-child>'."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating Custom Decorators"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can also create your own custom decorators to encapsulate reusable logic. A decorator is essentially a function that takes the target (class, property, method, or parameter) as an argument and can modify or add metadata to it."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"function Log(target: any, propertyKey: string, descriptor: PropertyDescriptor) {
  const originalMethod = descriptor.value;

  descriptor.value = function (...args: any[]) {
    console.log(`Calling ${propertyKey} with arguments: ${JSON.stringify(args)}`);
    const result = originalMethod.apply(this, args);
    console.log(`Method ${propertyKey} returned: ${result}`);
    return result;
  };

  return descriptor;
}

class MyClass {
  @Log
  myMethod(arg1: number, arg2: string): string {
    return `Result: ${arg1 + 10}, ${arg2}`;
  }
}

const instance = new MyClass();
instance.myMethod(5, 'hello');"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, 'Log' is a custom method decorator. It logs the method's arguments and return value to the console.  It takes three arguments: 'target' (the class prototype), 'propertyKey' (the name of the method), and 'descriptor' (the property descriptor)."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Benefits of Meta-programming in Angular"},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Code Reusability: Decorators allow you to encapsulate and reuse common logic across multiple components or services."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Improved Readability: Decorators provide a declarative way to add metadata and behavior to classes, making the code easier to understand."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reduced Boilerplate: Decorators can automate repetitive tasks, reducing the amount of boilerplate code you need to write."},{"order":26,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"Meta-programming with decorators is a powerful technique in Angular that allows you to write more concise, reusable, and maintainable code. By understanding and utilizing decorators effectively, you can significantly improve the structure and organization of your Angular applications."}]
